statement:
You have an integer array `nums` of length `n` and an array `changeIndices` of length `m`, both described with positions numbered from 1. Every value of `changeIndices` is a position of `nums` (between `1` and `n`). At the start no position of `nums` is marked, and the goal is to mark all of them.

Time runs in seconds `s = 1, 2, ..., m`. During second `s` you perform exactly one of these actions:

- pick any position `i` and lower `nums[i]` by 1;
- mark position `changeIndices[s]`, which is allowed only when `nums[changeIndices[s]]` is `0` at that moment;
- do nothing.

Return the smallest second `s` (between `1` and `m`) by whose end all positions can be marked if you choose actions optimally, or `-1` if that cannot be done within the `m` seconds. In code the arrays are 0-based: position `i` is `nums[i - 1]` and second `s` reads `changeIndices[s - 1]`, whose values stay 1-based.

validator:
function validate([nums, changeIndices]) {
  if (!Array.isArray(nums) || nums.length < 1 || nums.length > 2000) return 'nums needs 1 to 2000 values.';
  if (nums.some(v => !Number.isInteger(v) || v < 0 || v > 1e9)) return 'Each value of nums is an integer from 0 to 10^9.';
  if (!Array.isArray(changeIndices) || changeIndices.length < 1 || changeIndices.length > 2000) return 'changeIndices needs 1 to 2000 values.';
  if (changeIndices.some(v => !Number.isInteger(v) || v < 1 || v > nums.length)) return 'Each value of changeIndices is a position from 1 to nums.length.';
  return null;
}

